WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.260
Narrator: One of the most
famous costumes in cinema

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.613
is this plain white tank top

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.830
worn by Bruce Willis in "Die Hard."

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:18.640
But a lot of the damage you see on it

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.410
didn't just happen naturally.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:21.650
It was deliberately faked

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.546
by the film's costume
designer, Marilyn Vance.

00:00:24.546 --> 00:00:27.015
John: Now I know what
a TV dinner feels like.

00:00:27.015 --> 00:00:29.100
Narrator: Today, there's
a job specifically for

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.013
aging and destroying costumes
as described in the plot.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.840
Breakdown artists like Sarah Blostein

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.340
need an intimate
understanding of the scene

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.847
and countless creative ways to recreate

00:00:39.847 --> 00:00:42.117
all the damage a costume endures.

00:00:42.117 --> 00:00:44.327
When done right, they might just create

00:00:44.327 --> 00:00:46.680
an iconic piece of cinema history

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.573
that ends up in the Smithsonian.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.900
A lot of what we do, the
breakdown is from use.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.750
Narrator: Every piece of
clothing on a movie set

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:59.150
requires some sort of manipulation.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:03.440
Even a simple white shirt
gets bathed in dyes or tea.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.430
But breakdown artists go far beyond that,

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:08.422
like Sarah's work on "The Boys."

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.847
On set, the gunshot impacts were created

00:01:11.847 --> 00:01:14.300
by putting squibs under
the actor's jacket,

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.340
which left holes in the costume.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:19.497
But these tears looked nothing
like real bullet holes.

00:01:19.497 --> 00:01:20.532
The problem with this is that

00:01:20.532 --> 00:01:23.660
it causes the fabric to blow outwards.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.630
It would never do something like this.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:28.120
Narrator: One way to tackle
this is to sew the holes up.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.250
Sarah: A bullet hole actually

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.170
looks like a cigarette burn.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.550
The size of squib that they used

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:37.630
made gigantic, bazooka-size
holes in her jacket.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.501
Narrator: So off set, the breakdown team

00:01:39.501 --> 00:01:41.280
made the bullet holes from scratch,

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.140
using a craft knife to guarantee

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.290
the holes lined up precisely.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:48.070
Breakdown artists can
also emphasize details

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:51.940
that aren't necessarily realistic
but stand out on camera.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:55.010
Sarah: There is a certain
amount of movie-magic lying

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.330
that you have to do when
you do create a bullet hole

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.890
and make it a bit more
recognizable to the audience.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.340
Narrator: Like adding a little black paint

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.470
around the edges to mimic gun powder.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:06.845
Some people misinterpret
this look of bullet wipe

00:02:06.845 --> 00:02:08.680
and think that it's actually gun powder.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.530
You wouldn't get gun-powder
residue on a shot

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:16.170
unless the muzzle was directly
up against the person.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:17.880
Narrator: There are a
number of tools you can use

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.048
to create smaller rips
and tears from fights.

00:02:21.048 --> 00:02:23.660
Sarah's favorite tool: a steak knife.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:27.390
But it's less easy to control,
meaning less precision.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:29.700
Since she'll have to make
multiple identical versions

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.360
of the same outfit as backup,

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:35.010
craft knives and seam rippers
are better for consistency.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:36.160
When it came to "The Strain,"

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.520
she had to adjust for
this apocalyptic scenario.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.320
So she needed tools like a file,

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:43.327
a rougher,

00:02:44.977 --> 00:02:48.122
and a wire brush for
more destructive tears.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.430
Some outfits, like this
jacket from "The Strain,"

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:54.110
are made of materials
that are harder to rip.

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:56.900
For this, she'll have
to use a cheese grater.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:58.200
I really only use this

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:02.080
on things that will not
break down any other way.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.180
Narrator: It's not only rips and tears.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.440
There are a bunch of different ways

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.080
to represent wounds and blood,

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.100
and Sarah has to decide what
techniques work for the scene.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:13.140
In "The Boys," the characters
crash into a whale.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.910
The special effects team was tasked

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:17.340
with most of the blood and guts on set,

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.290
but the breakdown team
had to cycle through

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.073
several bloody outfits.

00:03:21.073 --> 00:03:22.540
Their biggest challenge?

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.080
Getting the color of blood
right to maintain continuity.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:27.940
When the characters first hit the whale,

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.350
the blood stains are a
brighter shade of red.

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:33.270
As it dries, it appears much darker.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:34.680
But the breakdown team couldn't let

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.490
the fake blood dry on its own,

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.400
as they ended shooting the later scenes

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.090
in the episode first.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:43.023
That meant separate shirts
for fresh and aged blood

00:03:43.023 --> 00:03:45.610
with the stains lined up precisely.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:47.010
Sarah also had to factor in

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.690
the different ways blood reacts

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:50.570
to fabrics and other props.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:55.120
Sarah: The only time blood would
really make drips like that

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:56.840
is on something that's plastic.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:01.200
If it's on fabric, it tends to run in.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.740
Sometimes I find it's more
realistic just to use your hands.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.520
Narrator: For something like whale guts,

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.600
the material matters as much as the color.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.440
For the fresher guts, she
needed something chunky

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:15.170
that would stick to
clothes and only look wet.

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:19.200
The silicone will kind of form
to whatever is underneath.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.270
So if you do it flat,

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:23.240
you're going to have a flat-looking shirt.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.400
Narrator: Which is why it's
best to use a mannequin.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.900
Sarah: It will keep that kind of wet,

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:30.766
clingy fabric look.

00:04:30.766 --> 00:04:31.640
When we were doing this,

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:35.000
we'd start with a brush or with a stick

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.750
and then just give up and
use our hands [laughs],

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:41.542
because honestly you're
just smushing stuff all over

00:04:41.542 --> 00:04:45.533
and you don't really have
to be artistic about it.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.590
So there's a nice gross piece up there.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:51.740
Narrator: For the later scenes
where the guts have dried,

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.500
Sarah switched to latex.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:56.830
It still had some shine
and clung to the fabric.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:58.020
Sarah: Just like the blood,

00:04:58.020 --> 00:04:59.930
when they're dried, they're darker,

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:01.780
they're kind of shriveled up.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:03.650
Narrator: Blood and rips are pretty common

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.230
breakdown scenarios for action projects,

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:07.990
but sometimes a scene will throw Sarah

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:09.750
a less common situation,

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:11.730
like for this scene in "Ready or Not."

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:15.033
Sarah had to think about how
clothes decompose on a body.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:19.190
She had to work on the
tuxedo on this corpse,

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:22.630
which had been buried
underground for decades.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:25.730
First, the clothing had
to reflect the context.

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:28.870
Sarah: So, this guy gets
shot with a bow and arrow,

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:31.470
but he's probably been running
around before this, right?

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:32.780
So he might have some rips.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:35.520
He might have some dirt,
definitely some sweat.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.660
Narrator: She used black
paint for the blood stains,

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:40.010
as they'd fully dried out and congealed.

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:42.260
Then she had to think about
how the bodily changes

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:44.030
would impact the outfit.

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:48.850
There's death, there's
bloating, there's liquefaction,

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:52.580
there's putrefaction, there's desiccation.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:54.670
I really wanted it to look like

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:57.100
whatever body juices seeped out into him,

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:58.820
seeped out into his clothes.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:01.520
Narrator: This body was
also buried in a deep pit.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:04.430
So Sarah mimicked the dry
rot caused by moisture

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.640
and added holes from
moths and other insects.

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:10.740
That's really where the
rougher and file were useful.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:13.632
To top it all off, because
the corpse was underground,

00:06:13.632 --> 00:06:15.100
it was covered in dirt.

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:16.500
And believe it or not,

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:18.730
recreating dirt is one of the

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:20.883
toughest challenges of the job.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:24.720
Using real dirt is really unpredictable,

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.000
as some dirt stains could be permanent,

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:28.680
a problem in the event that productions

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:31.750
do need to wash and reuse an outfit.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:35.250
So Sarah will replicate it
with nonpermanent materials.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:37.690
For the tuxedo, she painted on brown spots

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:41.510
and ground up nonpermanent
powder to create mud stains.

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:44.810
And for "The Boys," Sarah
had to adjust Kenji's clothes

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.340
after he brings down a
ceiling on Homelander

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:50.600
and later gets thrown
through a wall by Stormfront.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:53.970
She went beyond dirt powder
and added diatomaceous earth,

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.100
a powder that simulates the dust and dirt

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.180
you'd find in a tunnel.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:00.290
After Kenji gets thrown
through the drywall,

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:01.950
Sarah added this cellulose,

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:05.620
which is the same material
used for insulation in houses.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:08.210
It matched the set debris perfectly.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:11.150
Even the sweat stains you
see on-screen can be fake.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:13.310
This is especially true for action scenes

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:15.440
that are shot out of order.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:18.308
To simulate sweat stains
that stick and look wet,

00:07:18.308 --> 00:07:21.380
she sprays a mix of
glycerin, water, and oils.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:22.840
Sarah: It won't just run everywhere,

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:25.230
it'll kind of stay put
where it's supposed to be.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.550
Narrator: None of these
techniques we've mentioned so far

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:30.150
would work for these scenes
in "The Boys," however,

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.310
because of the supersuits.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:34.292
These costumes were expensive

00:07:34.292 --> 00:07:36.643
and shipped from LA to
Canada for the shoot,

00:07:36.643 --> 00:07:38.153
so the production team didn't have

00:07:38.153 --> 00:07:40.250
multiple versions to mess around with.

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:43.010
So how did Stormfront
and Black Noir's suits

00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:45.890
look so damaged here and here?

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:47.590
Sarah: So we had to come up with

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:49.140
unique and innovative ways

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:51.950
to make the costumes look
like they were damaged

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:53.820
without actually damaging them.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:55.480
Narrator: Sarah used leftover fabric

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:57.570
to recreate Stormfront's suit.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.050
That way the breakdown team
could rip it and sew in

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:03.000
prosthetic burn wounds created
by the makeup department.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.220
By painting a mushy mixture
around the edges of the rips,

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:08.660
Sarah could emulate real burn damage.

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:09.800
Sarah: If you look at a burn,

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:13.300
usually right around the lip of the burn,

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:16.560
there's a slightly lighter
color that's left there.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:18.260
And that gives the illusion

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:20.920
of the edge being singed
and kind of melted,

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:22.998
which is, I guess, what you would imagine

00:08:22.998 --> 00:08:26.050
for this kind of supersuit fabric.

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:27.230
Narrator: For Black Noir's suit,

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:30.290
the supersuits team
deconstructed it into panels,

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:32.390
allowing them to swap the original pieces

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:33.780
for pre-damaged ones

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:36.150
and then back again once filming ended.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:38.600
And look at the pieces of
shrapnel and melted bits

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:41.200
in Black Noir's suit following
a different explosion.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:45.350
Sarah: And then those were sewn
one by one onto the costume.

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:47.800
Narrator: So none of the
damage was permanent.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:50.700
Breakdown artists are a lot of things.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:54.323
They're scientists, they're
alchemists, they're detectives.

